data.content:
In .NET 10 (MAUI), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated/removed on Android because the underlying `adjustResize` mode was deprecated in Android 11+. The recommended approach is now **edge-to-edge rendering with `KeyboardInsets`**.

**Solution — use `KeyboardInsets` (Android):**

```xml
<!-- In your page XAML, wrap content in a Grid that responds to keyboard insets -->
<ContentPage xmlns:maui="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             SoftInputMode="AdjustNothing">
```

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs or your platform code:
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
{
#if ANDROID
    Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.WindowHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping("KeyboardInsets", (handler, view) =>
    {
        var activity = handler.PlatformView as Android.App.Activity;
        if (activity?.Window != null)
        {
            AndroidX.Core.View.WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(activity.Window, false);
        }
    });
#endif
});
```

Then in your page, use a `Grid` with padding that reacts to the keyboard:

```xml
<Grid>
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />

    <!-- Input bar at bottom -->
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

**Simplest alternative — just set `AdjustResize` via platform code:**

If you want the old behavior with minimal code, set it directly in `MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
    Window.SetSoftInputMode(Android.Views.SoftInput.AdjustResize);
}
```

> ⚠️ `AdjustResize` still works at runtime on most devices but is officially deprecated from API 30+. The insets-based approach is future-proof.

**For iOS**, the keyboard adjustment is handled automatically by MAUI's `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`. If it's not working, ensure you haven't disabled it:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs — make sure this is NOT set to true:
// Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect = true;
```

**TL;DR:** Replace `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` with either the Android WindowInsets API (recommended) or the direct `AdjustResize` call in `MainActivity.OnCreate` as a quick fix.
